ion had been  brought over the spanish language would  have likely dominated and guarani would  be limited to a rural indigenous  minority as happened with indigenous  languages elsewhere in latin america the  fact that they didn't see asuncion as  very important and neglected it probably  helped guarani to survive as a majority  language  jesuit missionaries also helped the  language survive they built jesuit  missions known as reductions a type of  settlement for indigenous guarani people  that operated autonomously from spanish  colonialist rule of course their goal  was colonialist in the sense that they  wanted to convert guarani people to  catholicism and european way of life but  they used the guarani language in their  efforts and they developed a system of  writing the language they also created a  standardized form of the language and  compiled guarani dictionaries grammars  and textbooks later when spain forced  the jesuit missionaries to leave  monolingual guarani speakers from the  missions migrated throughout the country  including to the cities  after paraguay gained its independence  from spain in 1811 the country's first  dictator used guarani as a nationalist  rallying cry and he also prohibited the  small number of spanish colonials from  marrying each other which essentially  resulted in forced race mixing and more  bilingualism  subsequent governments not only reversed  that coercive policy but they also  repressed the guarani language and  banned it in schools though governments  used guarani to inspire nationalistic  pride in times of war of course how  convenient  by the middle of the 20th century  populist politicians began speaking of  guarani more favorably to appeal to the  guarani speaking majority in 1967 it was  made a national language though spanish  remained the only official language and  in 1992 guarani was made an official  language alongside spanish  according to the 2012 census results  46.3 percent of households used both  guarani and spanish and 34 percent used  only guarani that means over 80 percent  of paraguayans speak guarani  the majority of guarani speakers use a  form of the language called  that involves heavy code switching with  spanish the word yopara means mixture  unlike the simple integration of spanish  loanwords referred to as  refers to actual code switching or code  mixing that includes elements of spanish  grammar the form of guarani used in  schools is not yo para but a more  traditional and literary form of the  language  let's take a look at some of the  features of guarani the orthography of  guarani is based on the latin  orthography used for spanish with a few  extra characters that represent guadalee  sounds that don't appear in spanish  all syllables consist of a vowel or a  consonant plus a vowel no syllables end  in consonants at least for native  guarani words for example mighty meaning  hello  meaning how are you and this is often  used as a greeting instead of maite  meaning fine or good  and you ipo  i'm fine too  guarani has six pairs of vowels six oral  vowels plus their nasal equivalents  all voiced stops and the one affricate  are pre-nasalized they only occur before  oral vowels their allophones are the  nasal consonants which occur before  nasal vowels replacing an oral consonant  with a nasal consonant to match adjacent  nasal sounds is an important feature of  guarani guarani has nasal harmony if  there's a stressed nasal vowel or a  nasal consonant its nasality spreads to  adjacent parts of the word this happens  with certain prefixes for example one  such prefix is the first person plural  inclusive subject pronoun prefix ja  which comes before an oral verb stem  before a nasal verb stem it becomes  so there's  meaning we laugh or we laughed and  there's  meaning we speak or we spoke so as a  little side note here guarani has a  distinction between we which includes  the listener and we which excludes the  listener  guarani is agglutinative meaning that  rather than adding additional words to a  sentence to show grammatical  distinctions multiple prefixes and  suffixes can be added instead here's an  example this phrase means see you later  the root is this verb  meaning c the subject is prefixed before  the verb root  we see then another prefix is added  between the subject prefix and verb root  to show reciprocation  we see each other then an adverb meaning  until is added after the verb  until we see each other or just see you  later  also nouns they take suffixes to show  number and case but not gender  this means cat  and this means cats  is an optional plural marker when it  comes after a nasal vowel it becomes  this means child  and this means children  the child loves the cat notice  at the end of the word for cat this is a  suffix that indicates the accusative  case for animate objects and it can also  show the locative case like for a  destination or location some other such  suffixes are we which indicates the  ablative case like from and  or  as a separate word which indicates  accompaniment like with  syntax notice the word order of this  sentence s-v-o this is the most common  word order in basic declarative  sentences but in reality the word order  is flexible and it's fairly common for  the subject to come after the verb and  for objects to come before the verb  notice that there's no definite article  no word meaning the there's no native  definite article in guadagni but the  spanish definite articles la and lo are  often used in daily conversation though  this would be considered yo para  possession what if we want to say the  child loves his cat or her cat  e is a prefix that functions as a  possessive adjective this one being  third person the other possessive  adjectives are separate words that  aren't prefixed like this  they're data this means your name a data  means name specifically a given name not  a family name  what's your name  my name is wan  when the possessor is an actual noun it  still comes before the thing that is  possessed  means my father's name  means my father and you already know  that veda means name  this means my father's name is jose  verbs guarani verbs feature something  called split intransitivity or an active  state of split transitive verbs are  active while intransitive verbs can be  either active or stative the active  verbs use active subject prefixes which  there are two different classes of while  the state of verbs use inactive subject  pronoun prefixes they're the same as the  possessives that we saw earlier and the  same as the standalone subject pronouns  except for the third person for example  i walk or i walked this is active  i remember or i remembered this is  stative notice that in addition to the  subject pronoun prefix that's attached  to the verb a standalone subject pronoun  is optionally used  but normally only to emphasize or  clarify stative verbs in guarani are  generally used for states or things that  happen that the person involved has no  control over and active verbs are  generally used for intentional actions  under the control of the subject but in  practice there are plenty of exceptions  so you really need to memorize which  verbs are active versus stative  grammatically speaking there's really no  distinction between state of verbs and  adjectives or nouns which can be used as  a predicate by adding an inactive  subject pronoun here's an example  i'm angry literally i anger here's  another example  i have a dog literally i dog this could  also be expressed with an independent  verb  in this case the verb is an active  transitive verb  tense and aspect in guarani when verbs  don't have any specific tense marking  they can be interpreted as either  present tense or past tense depending on  the context but there are also tense and  aspect markers that can come after the  verb for the distant past theirs  this means he lived in paraguay at some  point in the past  for the future tense 