he preceding vowel is nasal  another interesting feature of guarani  is its demonstratives words like this  these that and those in english there  are only two distinctions near and far  in guarani demonstratives have several  distinctions near  unmarked or middle distance far distance  absent or previously mentioned unseen  but audible or previously mentioned  this picture is beautiful this is the  demonstrative expressing that the  picture is near the speaker then there's  the word for picture  then we have the adjective  which is made predicative by adding an  inactive subject pronoun in this case  the third person  that picture is beautiful referring to a  picture that is not present when the  people are talking about it  earlier in the video i spoke briefly  about yo para but let's take another  look at it the degree to which people  mix guarani and spanish depends on the  person and the situation and it can  range from mostly guarani with some  spanish elements to mostly spanish with  some guarani elements older people and  people in the countryside tend to use  more traditional guarani and lestio para  while younger people and people in the  cities tend to use more job  here's an example of what yopara might  look like  this means i don't have that much  knowledge word for word it's i negative  i have negative that much knowledge  notice that the last two words are from  spanish  in traditional guarani this might be  which translates to i don't have so much  knowledge in this sentence all the words  are of guarani origin  or a more natural way of saying it might  be  which means i don't know so much  word for word it's i negative i know  negative so much  it's worth noting that many speakers and  proponents of more traditional guarani  have a distaste for job  and see it as a degraded form of the  language but the majority of speakers  speak some form of yobara  hopefully this video has given you an  interesting glimpse at the guarani  language unfortunately 